e game out of reach. GPT-6.1 Sol averaged 7.2 points lost per move with 9 blunders; the 20k averaged 4.8 with 5.", "moments": [{"turn": 9, "text": "D3 turned an even game (+1.0) into a deficit (-4.0), losing 5.0 points. The engine preferred B5. It was the first big slip of the game, and the next several Black moves lost more."}, {"turn": 17, "text": "F7 dropped Black from -27.1 to -44.7, a 17.6-point loss, the heaviest in a run of moves from turn 11 to 17 that each cost 6 to 18 points. The engine wanted F6 here. After this the game was effectively decided."}, {"turn": 29, "text": "G6 extended the wall on the right and fell from -51.2 to -85.2, losing 33.9 points. The engine's choice was B5 on the other side of the board."}, {"turn": 30, "text": "The 20k returned the favour with H6, which lost 34.1 points and brought the score back from -85.2 to -51.0. The engine preferred B4. Black had its chance to profit and did not."}, {"turn": 37, "text": "H4 was the worst move of the game, losing 34.4 points and sinking Black from -51.3 to -85.6. The engine's first choice was B5. By the final position White held a line of stones from B5 to J5, and Black's later passes and corner moves barely changed the score."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "The `inspect`/`check` function and the replay script only parse the board text, list chains, liberties and empty regions, apply one named move to report captures and suicide, and replay the finished game to verify the result. Every candidate move was chosen by hand. Nothing looped over empty points to rank moves or played a sequence forward, and the other commands only read notes and wrote game logs.", "stop": false}}